        "elapsedMs": 14921,
        "text": "Absolutely—since you’ve mentioned enjoying **Adobe Premiere Pro**, I’d start with these resources:\n\n### Premiere Pro skills\n- **[Adobe Learn](https://www.adobe.com/learn/premiere-pro)** — Free official tutorials covering editing fundamentals, audio, transitions, and color correction. A reliable starting point for **Lumetri Color and scopes**.\n- **[Premiere Gal](https://www.youtube.com/@PremiereGal)** — Practical tutorials on Premiere workflows, effects, captions, and audio. Many lessons are free on YouTube.\n- **[LinkedIn Learning](https://www.linkedin.com/learning/)** — Structured Premiere Pro courses if you prefer learning in sequence. Requires a subscription, though some public libraries provide access.\n\n### Storytelling and editing technique\n- **[This Guy Edits](https://www.youtube.com/@ThisGuyEdits)** — Excellent for understanding *why* a cut works: pacing, emotion, performance, and storytelling, rather than just software controls.\n- ** *In the Blink of an Eye* by Walter Murch** — A short, thoughtful book about choosing where to cut and putting emotion first. Useful regardless of which editor you use.\n\n### Color grading\n- **[Blackmagic Design’s training](https://www.blackmagicdesign.com/products/davinciresolve/training)** — Free videos, guides, and project files for DaVinci Resolve. You’ll need Resolve to follow the exercises, but concepts such as scopes, shot matching, and color balance also apply in Premiere.\n\n**A good way to start:** make a 30–60-second video from your own footage. Do separate passes for story, sound, and color, using tutorials to solve specific problems as you go.\n\nAre you more interested in cinematic storytelling, videos for Viowear, or developing your technical editing skills?"
